package wosh:ssh-core;

/// The sans-I/O SSH client core: `golang.org/x/crypto/ssh` (Go,
/// well-vetted, in wide production use) wrapped as a pure byte-and-tick
/// machine. This component performs NO I/O and holds NO keys: the
/// embedder (the `wosh-client` Rust component) owns the wire, the
/// clock, the connection string, and the identity, and drives this
/// core by feeding inbound bytes, draining outbound bytes, and
/// answering the two park-and-poll surfaces (signatures, prompts).
///
/// EVERY export is a plain synchronous `func`, deliberately: this
/// component imports nothing asynchronous, so none of componentize-go's
/// async-lifting hazards apply -- no keepalive task, no task-exit
/// races, no export that must not block. Internally the ssh stack runs
/// on goroutines that park on in-memory pipes and channels; parked
/// goroutines survive across export calls (measured, wosh M7), and
/// every state change happens inside one of these calls. x/crypto's
/// client path is timer-free, so the core never needs liveness the
/// embedder didn't cause.
///
/// Progress contract: any call that feeds this core (`feed`,
/// `answer-prompts`, `provide-signature`, `confirm-host-key`,
/// `authenticate-*`, `write-input`, `resize`) may produce outbound
/// bytes and state changes. After any such call, the embedder drains
/// `drain` to the wire and re-reads `status`. `pump` runs scheduler
/// rounds without new input, for the rare mid-handshake continuation
/// that needs another tick.
interface core {
  /// Where the session currently stands.
  variant status {
    /// The transport handshake is running on fed bytes.
    connecting,
    /// Key exchange finished: `host-key-sha256` is readable and the
    /// handshake is parked until `confirm-host-key` resolves it.
    /// Nothing has been sent to the server yet -- not a password, not
    /// even a public-key probe. That ordering is structural:
    /// x/crypto/ssh authenticates strictly after a successful
    /// host-key callback.
    host-key-check,
    /// The host key was accepted; authentication is in flight.
    authenticating,
    /// Authentication is parked on a signature request: read
    /// `pending-signature`, produce the signature, call
    /// `provide-signature` (or `fail-signature`).
    signing,
    /// Authentication is parked on a server-issued prompt batch: read
    /// `pending-prompts`, collect answers, call `answer-prompts`.
    auth-prompts,
    /// Pty and shell are up; `write-input`/`drain-output` carry the
    /// interactive session.
    ready,
    /// Terminal state: the handshake, auth, or shell failed, or the
    /// session ended. The string is a human-readable reason.
    closed(string),
  }

  /// One keyboard-interactive prompt: the text to display, and whether
  /// the user's answer should be echoed as typed (`false` means mask
  /// it, password-style).
  record prompt {
    text: string,
    echo: bool,
  }

  /// One server-issued batch of prompts (RFC 4256). The server
  /// drives: any number of batches, each with any number of prompts.
  /// `authenticate-auto`'s password round rides the same shape as a
  /// one-prompt masked batch.
  record prompt-batch {
    /// Free-text preamble to display above the prompts. Often empty;
    /// PAM setups use it for OTP guidance and expiry warnings.
    instruction: string,
    prompts: list<prompt>,
  }

  /// A public key offered for publickey authentication, in the two
  /// parts SSH actually puts on the wire.
  ///
  /// This core is deliberately ignorant of key ALGEBRA: it never
  /// parses `blob`, never holds a private half, and cannot check a
  /// signature it relays. It only needs the two strings RFC 4252 s7
  /// asks of it -- so a new algorithm is an embedder change, not a
  /// change here.
  ///
  /// The two fields are usually the same name, which is why it is
  /// worth spelling out that they are not the same THING. `algorithm`
  /// names the SIGNATURE and rides the userauth request (and the blob
  /// that gets signed); `blob` encodes the KEY and carries its own
  /// name inside. OpenSSH's browser-webauthn algorithm is the case
  /// that separates them: a plain `sk-ecdsa-sha2-nistp256@openssh.com`
  /// key blob -- exactly what sits in `authorized_keys` -- is offered
  /// under the algorithm `webauthn-sk-ecdsa-sha2-nistp256@openssh.com`,
  /// because the bytes the browser signs are shaped by WebAuthn rather
  /// than by SSH. Getting that pairing backwards is rejected by sshd
  /// (it resolves the algorithm name and compares it against the name
  /// inside the signature), so the split is load-bearing, not cosmetic.
  record public-key {
    /// The public key algorithm name for the userauth request: the
    /// name the SIGNATURE will carry.
    algorithm: string,
    /// The public key blob (RFC 4253 s6.6): length-prefixed name
    /// followed by algorithm-specific fields. Opaque here.
    blob: list<u8>,
  }

  /// A finished signature, in the three parts an SSH signature blob is
  /// built from -- `string format`, `string blob`, then whatever the
  /// algorithm appends.
  ///
  /// `trailer` exists for the security-key algorithms, which hang
  /// extra fields off the end of the standard two (authenticator
  /// flags, the signature counter, and for webauthn the origin and
  /// clientData the browser signed). It is appended verbatim, already
  /// SSH-encoded by whoever produced the signature; for every ordinary
  /// algorithm it is empty.
  record signature {
    /// The signature algorithm name, which must be the `algorithm` of
    /// the key this signature answers for.
    format: string,
    /// The algorithm's own signature encoding (for Ed25519 the raw 64
    /// bytes; for ECDSA an `mpint r, mpint s` pair).
    blob: list<u8>,
    /// Extra algorithm-specific fields, SSH-encoded, appended after
    /// `blob`. Empty for everything but the security-key algorithms.
    trailer: list<u8>,
  }

  /// A parked signature request: the bytes to sign, and the key they
  /// are to be signed for.
  ///
  /// The key rides along because an embedder may offer more than one
  /// (a browser key and a passkey, say), and only it knows which
  /// keeper holds which private half. Matching on `key` is exact:
  /// it is one of the records handed to `authenticate-publickey` or
  /// `authenticate-auto`, echoed back.
  record sign-request {
    /// Which offered key the server accepted and now wants proof of.
    key: public-key,
    /// The SSH publickey-auth signature blob (session id, user,
    /// service, algorithm, public key) to sign, verbatim. Not a hash:
    /// hashing, if the algorithm wants any, belongs to the signer.
    data: list<u8>,
  }

  /// The outcome of a finished probe (see `probe-start`).
  record probe-result {
    /// The command's exit status, when the server reported one; none
    /// when the channel closed without one (a vanished server, a
    /// signal).
    exit-status: option<s32>,
    /// Everything the command wrote, stdout and stderr interleaved in
    /// arrival order, capped at 256 KiB: a probe is a question, not a
    /// transfer, and a runaway answer is truncated rather than
    /// buffered without bound.
    output: list<u8>,
  }

  /// Where a bulk channel stands (see `resource channel`).
  variant channel-state {
    /// Both directions live -- meaning "not yet failed", not
    /// "granted": a channel is born `open` while the server's answer
    /// to the open request is still in flight, so a refusal shows up
    /// as a transition to `closed`, not as an open that never was.
    open,
    /// The remote sent EOF: it will produce no more bytes. Anything
    /// already buffered is still drainable -- `eof` is a statement
    /// about the REMOTE, not about this side's buffer, so drain until
    /// empty before concluding the reply is complete.
    eof,
    /// Terminal: the channel closed, failed, or died with its
    /// connection. The string is a human-readable reason. Buffered
    /// bytes are gone; a channel never leaves this state.
    closed(string),
  }

  resource session {
    /// Start an SSH client session whose wire is `feed`/`drain`.
    /// Never fails synchronously -- watch `status`. `user` is needed
    /// up front (x/crypto/ssh snapshots its config before the
    /// handshake) but is only ever SENT inside an auth request, which
    /// cannot happen before the host-key gate resolves. `cols`/`rows`
    /// size the pty requested once authenticated. The client version
    /// banner is drainable immediately after construction.
    ///
    /// `command`, when present, is sent as an SSH `exec` request in
    /// place of the default `shell` request once authentication
    /// succeeds (RFC 4254 s6.5) -- same channel, same pty, same I/O
    /// plumbing either way. The server runs it through the user's
    /// login shell, so $SHELL-style expansion happens there, not
    /// here. The purpose is create-or-attach session managers (`dtach
    /// -A`, `tmux new -AD`, `abduco -A`): create-or-attach semantics
    /// live entirely in the command, so this core needs no
    /// fresh-vs-reattach state of its own. `exit-status` then reports
    /// the command's exit, which for a session manager is how IT
    /// ended (detach or inner exit), not the inner shell's status.
    connect: static func(user: string, cols: u16, rows: u16, command: option<string>) -> session;

    // --- the byte plane ------------------------------------------

    /// Bytes that arrived from the wire.
    feed: func(data: list<u8>);

    /// Bytes to write to the wire: everything the ssh stack has
    /// produced since the last drain, possibly empty. Drain after
    /// every state-changing call (see the interface doc).
    drain: func() -> list<u8>;

    /// Signal that the wire is dead (EOF or transport error). The
    /// session moves to `closed` with `reason` once internal
    /// goroutines observe it; no further `feed` is expected.
    wire-broken: func(reason: string);

    /// Run scheduler rounds without feeding bytes, giving parked
    /// goroutines a chance to advance.
    pump: func();

    // --- the control plane (park-and-poll) -----------------------

    status: func() -> status;

    /// The server's host key fingerprint, in the form `ssh-keygen
    /// -lf` prints (`SHA256:...`), readable from `host-key-check`
    /// onward.
    host-key-sha256: func() -> option<string>;

    /// The verdict on that fingerprint. Rejecting fails the handshake
    /// with no credentials ever sent. Accepting is latched
    /// SYNCHRONOUSLY (visible to `authenticate-*` calls the moment
    /// this returns): the handshake proceeds once a credential is
    /// also on offer, in whichever order the two arrive.
    confirm-host-key: func(accept: bool);

    /// Offer a password. Fails if the host key has not been confirmed
    /// -- credentials are never sent to an unapproved server. Latches
    /// the credential and returns; authentication runs on subsequent
    /// `feed`/`pump` ticks, so poll `status`.
    authenticate-password: func(password: string) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Offer publickey auth for `keys`, in order. The private halves
    /// are deliberately NOT in this component: when the ssh stack
    /// needs a signature it parks with `status` = `signing`, the
    /// embedder answers via `provide-signature`, and only public
    /// halves and finished signatures ever cross this interface. Same
    /// host-key gating and latch-then-poll contract as
    /// `authenticate-password`.
    ///
    /// Offering several keys costs at most one ceremony: each is first
    /// OFFERED unsigned, and only a key the server says it will accept
    /// is ever signed for. A key whose algorithm the server does not
    /// support is skipped without parking. Fails synchronously on an
    /// empty list -- an offer of nothing is a caller mistake, not an
    /// authentication attempt.
    authenticate-publickey: func(keys: list<public-key>) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Start keyboard-interactive auth (RFC 4256): the server drives
    /// prompt batches; each parks the session at `auth-prompts` until
    /// `answer-prompts`. Zero-prompt batches are answered internally
    /// without surfacing. Same gating and latch-then-poll contract.
    authenticate-interactive: func() -> result<_, string>;

    /// Let the server steer method selection like a stock ssh client:
    /// publickey (with `keys`, when any are supplied) first, then
    /// password, then keyboard-interactive, constrained to what the
    /// server says can continue. Signatures park at `signing`; a
    /// password request surfaces as a one-prompt masked batch at
    /// `auth-prompts`; OTP rounds surface as their own batches. Same
    /// gating and latch-then-poll contract. An empty `keys` simply
    /// declines to offer publickey.
    authenticate-auto: func(keys: list<public-key>) -> result<_, string>;

    /// The signature request authentication is parked on, present
    /// while `status` is `signing`.
    pending-signature: func() -> option<sign-request>;

    /// Resume authentication with a signature over exactly the bytes
    /// `pending-signature` returned. Fails if no signature is pending,
    /// or if `format` is not the pending key's algorithm -- sshd
    /// rejects that mismatch several round trips later, with a message
    /// that says nothing useful, so it is caught here instead.
    provide-signature: func(sig: signature) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Abandon a pending signature (the signer refused or is
    /// unavailable) so authentication fails legibly instead of
    /// parking forever.
    fail-signature: func(reason: string);

    /// The prompt batch authentication is parked on, present while
    /// `status` is `auth-prompts`.
    pending-prompts: func() -> option<prompt-batch>;

    /// Answer the pending batch: one string per prompt, in order.
    /// Latches the answers and returns; the exchange continues on
    /// subsequent ticks. Fails if no batch is pending or the count
    /// does not match.
    answer-prompts: func(answers: list<string>) -> result<_, string>;

    // --- the terminal plane --------------------------------------

    /// Keystrokes or pasted bytes from the terminal. No local echo or
    /// line editing happens here -- the remote pty owns both. Bytes
    /// arriving before the shell is up are buffered and flushed to it
    /// in order.
    write-input: func(data: list<u8>);

    /// Pty output accumulated since the last call: raw bytes, in
    /// order, possibly empty.
    drain-output: func() -> list<u8>;

    /// Propagate a terminal resize.
    resize: func(cols: u16, rows: u16);

    /// True once the remote shell exited or the session closed.
    exited: func() -> bool;

    /// The shell's exit status, once known.
    exit-status: func() -> option<s32>;

    // --- the probe plane -------------------------------------------

    /// Run `command` on a SECOND session channel of the same
    /// authenticated connection -- the interactive channel above is
    /// not disturbed. No pty is requested for it, so its output comes
    /// back unmangled by tty cooking, which is what makes it
    /// parseable (installed session managers, existing sessions,
    /// whether anything is attached -- the kinds of questions this
    /// exists for).
    ///
    /// Only one probe runs at a time: starting one while `status` is
    /// not `ready`, or while a previous probe is still in flight (its
    /// result not yet observed via `probe-poll`), is an error.
    ///
    /// Like everything in this core, a probe advances on `feed`/`pump`
    /// ticks rather than inside this call, so poll `probe-poll` for
    /// the outcome. This core is timer-free by design -- x/crypto's
    /// client path never needs wall-clock help -- so any bound on how
    /// long a probe may run belongs to the embedder, not here.
    probe-start: func(command: string) -> result<_, string>;

    /// The outcome of the in-flight probe, if it has finished.
    ///
    /// The finished outcome is handed over exactly once: observing it
    /// here clears it, which is what makes the next `probe-start`
    /// legal. Returns `none` while the probe is still running, or when
    /// none was started (or the last one's result was already
    /// polled).
    probe-poll: func() -> option<probe-result>;

    // --- the bulk plane --------------------------------------------

    /// Open an SSH subsystem (RFC 4254 s6.5) on a NEW channel of the
    /// same authenticated connection, for carrying a byte protocol the
    /// embedder speaks and this core does not: `sftp` is the reason
    /// this exists. No pty is requested, so nothing cooks the bytes.
    ///
    /// Unlike the probe plane, which answers one question and hands
    /// back a capped buffer, a bulk channel is long-lived, streaming,
    /// and flow-controlled -- see `resource channel` for how
    /// backpressure works. Several may be open at once (an upload and
    /// a download are two), bounded only by what the server allows.
    ///
    /// Fails only on refusals decidable HERE, without the wire:
    /// `status` not `ready`, or the connection already gone. A
    /// SERVER's refusal is a round trip away and this core cannot
    /// block to hear it, so it arrives later, as
    /// `channel-state.closed(reason)` -- a server with no
    /// `Subsystem sftp` line surfaces there, with a reason naming the
    /// refused subsystem and the exec fallback. The embedder's move is
    /// protocol-shaped anyway: write the first request (for SFTP,
    /// INIT) and treat the reply-or-`closed` race as the verdict.
    open-subsystem: func(name: string) -> result<channel, string>;

    /// The same, but starting `command` through an `exec` request
    /// rather than naming a subsystem -- again with no pty.
    ///
    /// This is the fallback for an sshd that has no `Subsystem sftp`
    /// line: OpenSSH's `sftp-server` speaks its protocol on stdin and
    /// stdout regardless of how it was started, so
    /// `exec /usr/lib/openssh/sftp-server` reaches the same server the
    /// subsystem name would have. OpenSSH's own client does this for
    /// `sftp -s`. The channel that comes back is identical in every
    /// other respect.
    open-exec-channel: func(command: string) -> result<channel, string>;

    /// Tear the session down: fails any parked gate, closes the ssh
    /// stack, and moves to `closed`.
    close: func();
  }

  /// One bulk channel: a flow-controlled byte pipe on its own SSH
  /// channel, opened by `open-subsystem` or `open-exec-channel`.
  ///
  /// This core does not know or care what protocol runs over it. The
  /// embedder speaks SFTP; this end moves bytes and applies
  /// backpressure, exactly as the tunnel below it moves bytes without
  /// looking inside the SSH stream.
  ///
  /// Every method is synchronous and NONE of them block, which is what
  /// keeps this component free of componentize-go's async-lifting
  /// hazards even while carrying a transfer. Bytes move on `feed` and
  /// `pump` ticks like everything else here, so a transfer's
  /// throughput is bounded by how often the embedder ticks this core:
  /// a loop that drains until empty and pumps whenever the wire has
  /// bytes will saturate; a fixed poll interval will not. (At one
  /// 32 KiB SSH packet per tick, a 100 ms cadence caps a transfer near
  /// 320 KB/s no matter how fast the tunnel is.)
  ///
  /// FLOW CONTROL, and why it is shaped this way. A terminal needs
  /// none -- a human types slower than any buffer fills, which is why
  /// `drain-output` above simply hands over everything. A transfer
  /// inverts that: the far end will send as fast as its window allows,
  /// and a browser writing to storage is often slower. Unbounded
  /// buffering here would grow this component's heap without limit,
  /// so instead:
  ///
  /// * `write` reports how many bytes it ACCEPTED. A short write --
  ///   including zero -- means the outbound buffer is full and the
  ///   embedder must retry the remainder later. That is the entire
  ///   backpressure signal in this direction; there is no credit
  ///   protocol to get out of step with.
  /// * `drain` takes a `max`, so the embedder sets the chunk size and
  ///   therefore the pace. Inbound bytes are buffered up to a fixed
  ///   cap; while that cap is reached this core stops reading from the
  ///   SSH channel, x/crypto stops returning window adjustments, and
  ///   the server stops sending. Backpressure chains from the
  ///   embedder's storage all the way to sshd without any explicit
  ///   signal crossing this interface.
  ///
  /// CANCELLATION is free, deliberately. Since no method blocks and no
  /// future is ever in flight, abandoning a transfer means no more
  /// than ceasing to call `write`/`drain` and then calling `close`.
  /// Nothing is dropped mid-flight, so this plane sidesteps the
  /// no-cancel discipline the embedder must keep around its own async
  /// imports -- and a user who taps Cancel on a half-finished upload
  /// gets exactly that, with no torn state.
  ///
  /// LIFETIME. A channel belongs to the session that opened it but is
  /// not owned by it: closing or losing the session moves every
  /// channel to `closed`, after which each method is a legal no-op
  /// (`drain` returns empty, `write` errors) rather than a trap.
  /// Dropping the resource closes the channel.
  resource channel {
    /// Hand bytes to the far end. Returns how many were ACCEPTED,
    /// which may be fewer than offered, including zero -- retry the
    /// remainder after the next tick. Errors when the channel is no
    /// longer `open`, or after `finish` -- which is a promise this
    /// core holds the caller to: the SSH EOF is irrevocable, so bytes
    /// accepted after it could never be sent.
    write: func(data: list<u8>) -> result<u32, string>;

    /// Bytes the far end produced, at most `max`, in order.
    ///
    /// Empty means nothing is ready RIGHT NOW -- it is not an
    /// end-of-stream signal. Ask `state` for that, and note the
    /// ordering trap: drain until empty BEFORE treating `eof` as the
    /// end, or the tail of the reply is lost.
    drain: func(max: u32) -> list<u8>;

    /// Where this channel stands.
    state: func() -> channel-state;

    /// Send EOF: promise to write no more, while staying open to read
    /// what the far end still owes. This is what makes a server that
    /// waits for EOF before finishing (as `exec`-started helpers do)
    /// terminate cleanly instead of hanging.
    finish: func();

    /// Close the channel now, in both directions, discarding whatever
    /// is buffered. The session is untouched.
    close: func();
  }
}

world ssh-core {
  export core;
}
